  "notes": "⛔ **The one high-confidence entry on this axis, and the only one whose tempo, key, kit and melodic construction are all sourced rather than inferred.** The research gives 128 BPM straight in E♭ minor for the crossover record and \"an arpeggiated ostinato note sequence instead of a chord progression\" as the harmonic device — so `chords.impliedByRiff` stays true, the melody is a 16th arpeggio at [7, 12] notes a bar with `chordToneBias` 0.92, and `session.keys` weights D#m 6:1 over everything else. ⛔ **This model authors its own `modes`, which replaces the archetype's four**, and that is the correct behaviour rather than a loss: the research describes exactly two workflows and no others. `eurohouse crossover` pins 128 straight, turns `halfTime` off, puts the kick on all four with `secondaryAnchor` \"3\" and `syncopation` 0.06, moves the backbeat to 2&4, and opens the hat to `openHat.prob` 0.55 for the offbeat open — the \"Eurohouse aesthetic\" and the \"808 drum machines such as kick, sub bass, cymbals and handclaps\" the research quotes. `snap` is the rest of his catalog: half-time, snaps on 3, an organ riff instead of a saw pluck. ⚠ The one soft number is the second tempo band — his snap productions' 70–80 half-time is marked [LOW-CONF], so the `snap` mode's 140–160 is the inference while the crossover's 128 is documented. ⚠ `bass808.sustain` is `staccato` with a tight register because the research is explicit about why: \"short-to-medium decay so the 16th arpeggio stays legible\". A long 808 under a 16th arpeggio is the mistake this parameter exists to avoid. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts. ⚠ **The 1-bar phrase length was removed** (was [1,2], now [2,4]). A one-bar phrase looped four times carries less entropy than a four-bar phrase, and it is the countermelody it strangles hardest: a counter answers *inside* the melody’s phrase, so a one-bar shape leaves it nowhere to go. Every worst offender on `counter.rs` carried it. ⛔ The previous session recorded this exact mistake and it arrived again from the other direction — distinctness is combinatorial in the shape LENGTH, not in the option count.",
